#--: Overview

The declarations themselves live in #~"lib/system/cli.z", which is the one
place they are written. Every definition, field, arm, generic parameter and
own-line parameter there carries its own doc comment, and #~"zls" surfaces
each on hover; the corpus runner ratchets that coverage under #~"make ci".
This page is the prose and the worked examples -- read it for how the unit
is meant to be used, and the source for the exact surface.

The #~"cli" unit is a small argv parser. Users declare a
#~"Spec" (flags, options, positional arguments, each with help
text), hand it to #~"cli.parse" with the argv list, and get back
a #~"Parsed" handle with accessor methods. Help text is
auto-generated from the spec.

Accessed via qualified names only -- #~"cli.Spec.create",
#~"cli.parse ..."; no unqualified re-export.

#b:"Grammar supported (GNU-getopt conventions)."

#code language=text: """
  --name              long flag
  --name value        long option (value is the next argv token)
  --name=value        long option (embedded value)
  -x                  short flag
  -o value            short option
  -xvf                bundled short flags
  -vo value           bundle ending in a short option (value is the next token)
  -ofile              short option with embedded value
  --                  end-of-options; rest go to extraArgs
  -                   positional (stdin convention)
"""

#b:"Design principles."

#ul:
  [#*: #b:"Register-then-parse." Setup is a sequence of
    declarative calls against a mutable spec; the whole parse is
    one function call. No stateful builder objects to juggle]
  [#*: #b:"One argv shape." #~"cli.parse" takes the whole vector
    as #~"os.args" returns it and reads it from position
    #~"first", which is 1 when not given: position 0 is the
    program name. A program that reads its command at position 1
    itself gives #~"first: 2"]
  [#*: #b:"Fail fast, fail whole." Parse either returns a fully
    validated #~"Parsed" or an early #~"err" with a descriptive
    payload. No partial results; no mutable error list]
  [#*: #b:"Help is a property of the Spec." #~"cli.helpText"
    takes the same spec that parsed the args, so the rendered
    usage never drifts from the registered behaviour]
  [#*: #b:"One wording for an error." #~"cli.errorText" gives the
    sentence for a #~"CliError", so every program that uses the
    unit refuses a command line in the same words]

#b:"Not shipped yet."

#ul:
  [#*: Long-option prefix abbreviation (#~"--ver" for
    #~"--verbose"): v1 is exact-match only]


#--: Index

#p: #b:"Registration types"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=flagdef: #b:"FlagDef"] -- #~"class { name: String  shortName: String  help: String }"]
  [#*: [#> to=optiondef: #b:"OptionDef"] -- #~"class { name: String  shortName: String  help: String  required: bool  repeats: bool }"]
  [#*: [#> to=positionaldef: #b:"PositionalDef"] -- #~"class { name: String  help: String  required: bool }"]

#p: #b:"Spec + Parsed handle"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=spec: #b:"Spec"] -- #~"class { programName: String  summary: String  flags: ... options: ... positionals: ...  stopAtOperand: false }"]
  [#*: [#> to=parsed: #b:"Parsed"] -- #~"class { flagSet ...  optionValues ...  repeatValues ...  positionalValues ...  extraArgs ...  extraOperands: u64 }"]
  [#*: [#> to=optionvalue: #b:"OptionValue"] -- #~"class { name: String  value: String }"]

#p: #b:"Errors"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=clierror: #b:"CliError"] -- #~"union { unknownFlag: String ... unexpectedArg: String }"]

#p: #b:"Registration"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=add-flag: #b:"cli.addFlag"] -- #~"function {spec: Spec.borrow name: String.take shortName: String.take help: String.take}"]
  [#*: [#> to=add-option: #b:"cli.addOption"] -- #~"function {spec: Spec.borrow name: String.take shortName: String.take help: String.take required: bool repeats: false}"]
  [#*: [#> to=add-positional: #b:"cli.addPositional"] -- #~"function {spec: Spec.borrow name: String.take help: String.take required: bool}"]

#p: #b:"Parse + render"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=parse: #b:"cli.parse"] -- #~"function {spec: Spec.view args: (List String).take first: 1.u64} out (Result Parsed E: CliError)"]
  [#*: [#> to=help-text: #b:"cli.helpText"] -- #~"function {spec: Spec.view} out String"]
  [#*: [#> to=error-text: #b:"cli.errorText"] -- #~"function {spec: Spec.view e: CliError.view} out String"]


#--: flagdef


One registered long flag plus optional short alias. Class
because #~"String" fields are reftypes and valtypes cannot hold
reftypes.

Users rarely construct #~"FlagDef" directly -- the
#~"cli.addFlag" helper does it for you.


#--: optiondef


One registered option (long + optional short alias), with a
required-presence flag and a #~"repeats" flag. Constructed via
#~"cli.addOption".


#--: positionaldef


One registered positional slot, named for lookup. Constructed
via #~"cli.addPositional".


#--: spec


Parser registration state. Built incrementally via
#~"cli.Spec.create" then the #~"cli.addFlag" / #~"cli.addOption" /
#~"cli.addPositional" helpers. All string
fields are owned; list fields hold the registered defs in
insertion order (which is also help-text output order).

#code language=zerolang name=spec: """
  sp: cli.Spec.create programName: "zgrep".string summary: "search files for a pattern".string
"""

The three list fields are initialised empty.

#b:"#~\"stopAtOperand\"" is for a program that starts another one and
hands it arguments. Set it to #~"true", and the first operand past the
declared positionals ends the reading: that token and every token after
it go to #~"extraArgs" as written, whatever they look like. A flag
written before that operand is still read. #~"--" ends the reading as
it always does.

#code language=zerolang body="""
  main: function is {
      sp: cli.Spec.create programName: "tool".string summary: "".string
      sp.stopAtOperand = true
      cli.addFlag spec: sp name: "--verbose".string shortName: "-v".string help: "".string
      cli.addPositional spec: sp name: "unit".string help: "".string required: true
      args: List String
      for each t: ("tool hello -v one --two -v".split sep: " ") loop args.append t.string
      pr: cli.parse spec: sp :args
      match (
          pr
      ) case ok then {
          print "verbose \{pr.hasFlag name: "--verbose"}"
          for each x: pr.extraArgs loop print "extra \{x}"
      } case err then {
          print (cli.errorText spec: sp e: pr)
      }
  }
  """ expect="""
  verbose true
  extra one
  extra --two
  extra -v
  """


#--: parsed


Successful parse result. Opaque; inspect via the accessor
methods.

#b:"Accessors."

#ul:
  [#*: #~"hasFlag name" -- was this flag present? Look up by the
    registered long name (e.g. #~"--verbose"), not the short
    alias]
  [#*: #~"option name" -- value of this option, or
    #~"Option.none" if absent]
  [#*: #~"positional name" -- value of this positional, or
    #~"Option.none" if absent]
  [#*: #~"optionAll name" -- every value kept for this option, in
    the order written: all of them for an option declared
    #~"repeats: true", the last for any other. An empty list if
    absent]
  [#*: #~"given name" -- was this name written at all, as a flag,
    as an option or as a positional? It tells a value the command
    line gave from a default the program fills in]

#b:"#~\"extraArgs\"" holds every token beyond the declared
positionals, then every token after a bare #~"--" (the
end-of-options terminator), in order. There is no accessor -- read the
field directly.

#b:"#~\"extraOperands\"" is how many of #~"extraArgs" are operands
beyond the declared positionals. The tokens from that index on were not
read: they followed #~"--", or the first of them ended the reading under
#~"stopAtOperand". A program that hands the tokens after #~"--" to
another program, and takes no operand of its own before it, reads the
two parts apart:

#code language=zerolang body="""
  main: function is {
      sp: cli.Spec.create programName: "tool".string summary: "".string
      cli.addPositional spec: sp name: "unit".string help: "".string required: true
      args: List String
      for each t: ("tool hello stray -- one --two".split sep: " ") loop args.append t.string
      pr: cli.parse spec: sp :args
      match (
          pr
      ) case ok then {
          i: 0.u64
          for each x: pr.extraArgs loop {
              if i < pr.extraOperands then print "operand \{x}" else print "passed on \{x}"
              i = i + 1
          }
      } case err then {
          print (cli.errorText spec: sp e: pr)
      }
  }
  """ expect="""
  operand stray
  passed on one
  passed on --two
  """

#b:"Ownership." #~"option" / #~"positional" each return a
fresh #~"Option String" -- scope-exit frees the boxed string.
#~"optionAll" returns a fresh #~"List String".


#--: optionvalue


One value written for an option that repeats: the option's long
name and the value. #~"Parsed.repeatValues" holds them in the order
written; read them through #~"optionAll".


#--: clierror


Parse-time failures. Each arm carries a descriptive #~"String"
so callers can compose diagnostics or print the offending token
directly. Union (not variant) because every arm carries a
reftype payload.

#b:"Arms."

#ul:
  [#*: #~"unknownFlag" -- an argv token starts with #~"-" or
    #~"--" but no registered flag / option matches, or a bundle
    such as #~"-vq" holds a letter that none does. Payload is
    the offending token]
  [#*: #~"missingValue" -- an option's expected value was
    absent (e.g. #~"--output", or a bundle ending in one such as
    #~"-vo", with no following token). Payload is the token as
    given (#~"--output", #~"-o" or #~"-vo")]
  [#*: #~"missingRequired" -- an option or positional marked
    #~"required: true" had no value. Payload is the name]
  [#*: #~"unexpectedArg" -- a flag given an embedded value
    (#~"--verbose=1"); flags take none. Payload is the token]


#--: add_flag


Register a flag on #~"Spec". The spec is borrowed so mutations
propagate to the caller's spec. Name conventions: pass the full
long form (#~"--verbose") and optional short alias
(#~"-v"); help text is free-form.

#code language=zerolang after=spec: """
  (
      cli.addFlag
      spec: sp
      name: "--verbose".string
      shortName: "-v".string
      help: "verbose output".string
  )
"""


#--: add_option


Register a value-carrying option on #~"Spec". #~"required: true"
causes a #~"missingRequired" error from #~"parse" when the
option is absent. #~"repeats: true" keeps every value the command
line gives, for #~"optionAll"; it is #~"false" when not given, and
#~"option" answers the last value either way.

#code language=zerolang after=spec: """
  (
      cli.addOption
      spec: sp
      name: "--output".string
      shortName: "-o".string
      help: "out file".string
      required: false
  )
  (
      cli.addOption
      spec: sp
      name: "--include".string
      shortName: "-I".string
      help: "a directory to search; may be given again".string
      required: false
      repeats: true
  )
"""


#--: add_positional


Register a positional slot on #~"Spec". Positionals are
consumed in registration order as argv tokens arrive.

#code language=zerolang after=spec: """
  cli.addPositional spec: sp name: "pattern".string help: "regex".string required: true
  cli.addPositional spec: sp name: "file".string help: "input".string required: true
"""


#--: parse


Parse #~"args" against the registered #~"Spec". #~"args" is the
whole argument vector as #~"os.args" returns it; position 0, the
program name, is skipped. Typical call site:

#code language=zerolang after=spec: """
  pr: cli.parse spec: sp args: os.args
  match (
      pr
  ) case ok then {
      v: pr.hasFlag name: "--verbose"
      o: pr.option name: "--output"
      pat: pr.positional name: "pattern"
      print "verbose=\{v}"
  } case err then {
      io.eprintln (cli.helpText spec: sp)
  }
"""

Accessors look up by the registered long name, not the short
alias -- use the long name throughout for both registration and
lookup.

#b:"A program with commands" reads its command at position 1
itself, picks that command's spec, and gives #~"first: 2" so that
#~"parse" reads what follows the command.

#code language=zerolang body="""
  main: function is {
      sp: cli.Spec.create programName: "tool".string summary: "".string
      (
          cli.addOption
          spec: sp
          name: "--src".string
          shortName: "".string
          help: "".string
          required: false
          repeats: true
      )
      args: List String
      for each t: ("tool build --src a --src=b main".split sep: " ") loop args.append t.string
      pr: cli.parse spec: sp :args first: 2
      match (
          pr
      ) case ok then {
          for each d: (pr.optionAll name: "--src") loop print "src \{d}"
          for each x: pr.extraArgs loop print "extra \{x}"
      } case err then {
          print (cli.errorText spec: sp e: pr)
      }
  }
  """ expect="""
  src a
  src b
  extra main
  """

#b:"Ownership." #~"parse" consumes #~"args" (the argument list
may be freed during parsing). Callers who need to retain the
argv vector should pass a copy. #~"Spec" is only read -- the
same spec can parse multiple argv vectors.


#--: help_text


Render the usage + help block from the spec. Returns a
freshly-allocated #~"String".

#code language=text: """
  usage: zgrep [options] <pattern> <file>

  zgrep-style pattern matcher.

  Flags:
    -v, --verbose    verbose output

  Options:
    -o, --output    out file

  Positionals:
    pattern    regex
    file    input
"""

Layout is deterministic: registration order is preserved.


#--: error_text


The sentence that says a #~"CliError" to the person who wrote the
command line. It starts with the spec's #~"programName", is one line,
and has no newline at its end.

#table cols="2 5":
  Error                |Sentence
  #~"unknownFlag"      |#~"tool: unknown option '--quiet'"
  #~"missingValue"     |#~"tool: --output needs a value"
  #~"missingRequired"  |#~"tool: missing --level" for an option, #~"tool: missing <file>" for a positional
  #~"unexpectedArg"    |#~"tool: --verbose takes no value"

A program that has a help command appends its own pointer to it;
#~"errorText" does not know that one exists.

#code language=zerolang body="""
  main: function is {
      sp: cli.Spec.create programName: "tool".string summary: "".string
      cli.addPositional spec: sp name: "file".string help: "".string required: true
      args: List String
      args.append "tool".string
      pr: cli.parse spec: sp :args
      match (
          pr
      ) case ok then {
          print "ok"
      } case err then {
          io.eprintln "\{cli.errorText spec: sp e: pr}; `tool help` lists the options"
          print (cli.errorText spec: sp e: pr)
      }
  }
  """ expect="""
  tool: missing <file>
  """
